      "version": 119,
      "versionNonce": 2030326323,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790856924463,
      "created": 1790856873962,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "BOARD/MANUAL\n+\nREASON",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "center",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "4pLZE4EziyQtn-GtKJWZy",
      "originalText": "BOARD/MANUAL\n+\nREASON",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": 0.41437148916853483
    },
    {
      "id": "kqC3r4B6E6AC0L0XHbOgx",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2342.930118060956,
      "y": 602.2629262912991,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#e9ecef",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0o",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1336522749,
      "version": 105,
      "versionNonce": 162202109,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790857039588,
      "created": 1790857019189,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Problem:\nImagine a boarding agent has a camera that says, “This face looks like\nMahmoud.” That is only a possible match. The gate still needs answers to four\nquestions:\n\n1. Is Mahmoud on this flight?\n2. Did he consent to biometric boarding, and has he already boarded?\n3. Is the match clear enough, or does another passenger look nearly as similar?\n4. If the camera, model, identity service, or database fails, how does boarding\ncontinue safely?\n\nREPO FACT: The research focused on these integration, matching, consent, and\nfallback problems. It does not reveal how any employer’s internal gate is built",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Problem:\nImagine a boarding agent has a camera that says, “This face looks like Mahmoud.” That is only a possible match. The gate still needs answers to four questions:\n\n1. Is Mahmoud on this flight?\n2. Did he consent to biometric boarding, and has he already boarded?\n3. Is the match clear enough, or does another passenger look nearly as similar?\n4. If the camera, model, identity service, or database fails, how does boarding continue safely?\n\nREPO FACT: The research focused on these integration, matching, consent, and fallback problems. It does not reveal how any employer’s internal gate is built",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "skY8W8barFrUUF3o2KylV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -2342.1787548684115,
      "y": 1049.2639365886903,
      "width": 800,
      "height": 425,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "#e9ecef",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0p",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1883860957,
      "version": 151,
      "versionNonce": 1851611293,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1790857377536,
      "created": 1790857267025,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How your POC solves it:\n\nPOC NOW: Before boarding, the gate service gets a flight manifest, takes\nreference images only for passengers with active consent, turns those images\ninto face embeddings, encrypts them, and stores a flight-specific gallery. \n\nDuring boarding, it compares a new face embedding with eligible entries in that\ngallery. It boards only when the score passes the threshold, the next-best\nmatch is far enough behind, and the mock identity provider says the selected\nprofile is valid. Otherwise, it gives staff a MANUAL decision with a reason.\n\nA made-up score example using the configured R50 rules: if the best score is 0.61\nand second-best is 0.44, the best clears the 0.44 threshold and the 0.17 gap\nclears the 0.10 margin. The gate then asks the mock identity provider to confirm\nthe profile’s status. If the scores are 0.61 and 0.57, the 0.04 gap is too close,\nso staff check the passenger manually. These example scores are for explanation;\nthey are not benchmark results",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "baseFontSize": null,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How your POC solves it:\n\nPOC NOW: Before boarding, the gate service gets a flight manifest, takes reference images only for passengers with active consent, turns those images into face embeddings, encrypts them, and stores a flight-specific gallery. \n\nDuring boarding, it compares a new face embedding with eligible entries in that gallery. It boards only when the score passes the threshold, the next-best match is far enough behind, and the mock identity provider says the selected profile is valid. Otherwise, it gives staff a MANUAL decision with a reason.\n\nA made-up score example using the configured R50 rules: if the best score is 0.61 and second-best is 0.44, the best clears the 0.44 threshold and the 0.17 gap clears the 0.10 margin. The gate then asks the mock identity provider to confirm the profile’s status. If the scores are 0.61 and 0.57, the 0.04 gap is too close, so staff check the passenger manually. These example scores are for explanation; they are not benchmark results",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "labelPosition": null
    },
    {
      "id": "5RGBD7dYG4g1OTjDMApet",
